"""Render the public methods, validation and six-slide pitch from checked artifacts."""
import gzip
import html
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
OUT = ROOT / 'dist'
META = json.loads((OUT/'metadata.json').read_text())
CAL = json.loads(gzip.decompress((OUT/'calibration.json.gz').read_bytes()))
H = html.escape

def page(title, content, css=''):
    return f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>{H(title)} | Ember Ledger</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">{css}</head><body><header class="masthead"><a class="brand" href="./"><span class="mark" aria-hidden="true">◈</span> EMBER<span class="brand-accent">LEDGER</span></a><div class="header-right"><a href="./">Open atlas ↗</a><a href="evidence.html">Project evidence ↗</a><a href="method.html">Methods ↗</a><a href="pitch.html">Project demo ↗</a></div></header>{content}</body></html>'''

sources=''.join(f'<li><a href="{H(s["url"])}">{H(s["sensor"])} · {s["year"]} · {s["observations"]:,} retained observations</a><small>Input version field: {H(", ".join(s["versionValues"]))}<br>SHA-256 {s["sha256"]}</small></li>' for s in META['sources'])
methods=f'''<main class="reading"><div class="kicker">DATA &amp; METHODS</div><h1>What the record can tell us.</h1><p class="intro">Ember Ledger combines an observed fire calendar with a separate experimental estimate of earlier activity. The distinction stays visible in the app and its downloads.</p>
<section><h2>01 · Data and coverage</h2><p>The atlas contains <strong>{META['retainedObservations']:,} retained observations</strong> from 39 NASA FIRMS United States annual files. MODIS covers 2001–2025. VIIRS Suomi NPP covers 2012–2025. The study window is 125°W–117.7°W and 36.6°N–42.8°N, centered on Northern California and including parts of neighboring states. The latest included date is December 31, 2025. No 2026 or live observations are included.</p><p>Inputs retain type 0 (presumed vegetation fire), MODIS confidence ≥30 and VIIRS nominal or high confidence. These are separate product filters, not equivalent confidence scores. MODIS has nominal 1 km pixels; VIIRS has nominal 375 m pixels. MODIS includes Terra and Aqua when available. The original version fields vary across annual files. We publish those values and hashes below rather than label all years as one collection.</p></section>
<section><h2>02 · One observed cell per UTC day</h2><p>Observation coordinates are assigned to a 2 km equal-area cylindrical grid anchored at 125°W and 36.6°N, using a spherical Earth of radius 6,371.0088 km and a standard parallel of 39.7°N. The 4 km and 8 km grids nest the 2 km cells. For a selected rectangle, base cells are included when their centers fall inside it.</p><p>A combined cell is active when either sensor has at least one retained observation on that UTC day. Shared cells indicate spatial agreement within a day; the observations can be hours apart. This union removes repeated counting at the chosen scale. It does not correct sensor sensitivity, missing coverage or differences in overpass time.</p></section>
<section><h2>03 · Calendar, colors and seasonal reference</h2><p>Monthly active-cell days are the sum of daily active-cell counts. A cell can contribute once each day, so these totals do not count separate fires or measure burned area. The heatmap shares a logarithmic color scale across all displayed years. The daily calendar can color by count or by the number of cells above its seasonal reference.</p><p>The reference uses 2017–2024. For each selected date, it gathers the same calendar date <strong>±7 days</strong> in those reference years, excludes the selected year, then takes the nearest-rank 95th percentile. February 29 uses February 28 as the anchor in non-leap years. Dates outside the reference years are excluded. This gives 105 comparison days for August 5, 2021. Reference years stay fixed when the archive is extended.</p><p>Days with no retained observations contribute zero, although missing observations and clouds are not modeled. A diamond means the observed union exceeds the reference. The comparison is retrospective and can use later years. Flags are unavailable for MODIS-only years. The 2012 record is a transition year and may have incomplete VIIRS coverage.</p></section>
<section><h2>04 · An experimental estimate for 2001–2011</h2><p>The adjusted-history view applies a seasonal count adjustment only to the <strong>full study window</strong>. It estimates the combined-observation count from MODIS. It does not infer individual hotspot coordinates. Terra-only observations supply 2001 and all of 2002; Terra+Aqua supply 2003–2011. The heatmap marks estimated months with ~ and EST. The map, cell evidence and ordinary CSV exports continue to show observed data.</p><p>Separate models are fitted for each grid size and early-era input. We tested them by leaving out one complete year at a time across 2013–2025. Historical estimates assume that the overlap-era relationship also applies to earlier years. That assumption cannot be checked against VIIRS before 2012. See the <a href="validation.html">validation results, formulas and limitations</a>.</p></section>
<section><h2>05 · Checks on the original Dixie example</h2><p>On August 5, 2021, the Dixie Fire study area contains 999 retained MODIS detections and 3,755 VIIRS detections. At 4 km they occupy 98 union cells, including 76 shared cells, against a reference of 15.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Grid</th><th>Observed union</th><th>Shared cells</th><th>Seasonal 95th</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 km</td><td>268</td><td>190</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>4 km</td><td>98</td><td>76</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>8 km</td><td>39</td><td>30</td><td>8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For 984 July–October days in 2017–2024, 109 were flagged at one or more grid sizes. Of these, 93 were flagged at all three sizes and 16 depended on the grid. This is a scale-sensitivity check, not a measurement of detection accuracy.</p></section>
<section><h2>06 · Downloads and reproducibility</h2><p>Choose a date range for daily analysis, including zero-observation days, or download retained source observations with original coordinates, acquisition times, confidence and version fields. Source files use the same base-cell-center area rule as the map, so the raw row totals match the displayed detection totals. Added source and grid columns are labeled explicitly. A separate download contains modeled full-region totals for 2001–2011, regardless of the selected custom area or dates. SVG export provides a labeled chart of the observed daily counts.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/Abhinav0905/ember-ledger">GitHub repository</a> includes the Python download and preparation pipeline, calibration experiment, tests, app code and rebuild instructions. No API key or LLM service is required to run the site.</p></section>
<section><h2>07 · Limits and next steps</h2><p>Clouds, smoke, viewing geometry, overpass schedules, processing changes and outages affect the record. Positive-detection files do not say whether a satellite had a clear view. An empty cell can still contain fire. Case-study rectangles are study areas, not incident perimeter masks, so detections are not individually attributed to the named fire.</p><p>Historical adjustment reduces disagreement with a combined observed reference for this fixed region. It does not make the sensors interchangeable everywhere. The reference shares MODIS inputs with the predictor and is not independent ground truth. Extending the model to custom areas, adding observation-coverage masks and testing against independent measurements remain future work. A future 2026 update must label the data cutoff and any change between archive and near-real-time products.</p></section>
<section><h2>Sources and AI use</h2><p>Data courtesy of NASA/LANCE FIRMS. ChatGPT/Codex assisted with prototype code, interface text, analysis and tests. All satellite observations come from the NASA archive; modeled totals are labeled separately.</p><ul class="references"><li><a href="https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/data/country/">NASA FIRMS annual country archive</a></li><li><a href="https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/data/country/AttributesReadme.txt">FIRMS CSV field definitions</a></li><li><a href="https://www.spaceappschallenge.org/2026/challenges/modis-and-viirs-hot-spots/">Official challenge brief</a></li><li><a href="https://modaps.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/services/production/outages_suomi_npp.html">NASA MODAPS Suomi NPP outage history</a></li><li><a href="https://landweb.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/data/userguide/MODIS_C6_Fire_User_Guide_C.pdf">MODIS Active Fire Product User Guide</a></li><li><a href="https://www.fire.ca.gov/incidents/2021/7/13/dixie-fire">CAL FIRE Dixie Fire incident</a></li><li><a href="{H(META['boundarySource'])}">State outlines via PublicaMundi Mapping API examples</a></li></ul><details class="archives"><summary>Original files, version fields and SHA-256 checksums</summary><ul class="references">{sources}</ul></details></section><footer><a href="./">Return to atlas ↗</a><a href="validation.html">Inspect validation ↗</a></footer></main>'''
(OUT/'method.html').write_text(page('Methods',methods))

rows=''
for grid in ('2','4','8'):
 for source in ('modis','terra'):
  model=CAL['grids'][grid]['models'][source];v=model['validation'];b=v['baseline'];m=v['model']
  rows+=f'<tr><td>{grid} km</td><td>{"Terra+Aqua" if source=="modis" else "Terra only"}</td><td>{b["mae"]:.2f}</td><td>{m["mae"]:.2f}</td><td>{v["maeImprovementPct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{v["yearsImprovedMAE"]}/{v["yearsTotal"]}</td></tr>'
folds=''.join(f'<tr><td>{f["year"]}</td><td>{f["baseline"]["mae"]:.2f}</td><td>{f["model"]["mae"]:.2f}</td><td>{f["model"]["bias"]:.2f}</td></tr>' for f in CAL['grids']['4']['models']['modis']['folds'])
v=CAL['grids']['4']['models']['modis']['validation'];sens=CAL['grids']['4']['models']['modis']['outageExcludedSensitivity']
validation=f'''<main class="reading"><div class="kicker">EXPERIMENTAL HISTORICAL ADJUSTMENT</div><h1>Test the gap between sensors.</h1><p class="intro">The question is narrow. Can MODIS counts better approximate the combined MODIS–VIIRS record when an entire year is withheld from fitting?</p><section><div class="validation-metric">{v['maeImprovementPct']:.1f}% lower daily MAE</div><p>Full study window, 4 km grid, Terra+Aqua input. Across 13 held-out years, the mean absolute error fell from <strong>{v['baseline']['mae']:.2f} to {v['model']['mae']:.2f} active cells per day</strong>. Every held-out year improved. The target is the observed cell union, so this measures internal agreement rather than independent fire-detection accuracy.</p></section>
<section><h2>How the model works</h2><p>For each grid size and input type, fit a multiplier through the origin on daily active-cell counts. Fit one slope for all seasons and one for each calendar quarter. Shrink each quarterly slope toward the all-season value when it has few active-input days.</p><div class="formula">global slope = sum(input × target) / sum(input²)<br>quarter weight = active input days / (active input days + 30)<br>quarter factor = max(1, weight × quarter slope + (1 − weight) × global slope)<br>estimated daily union = observed input cells × quarter factor</div><p>The target is the union of retained MODIS Terra+Aqua and VIIRS SNPP cells. Separate Terra-only models support 2001–2002. Combined MODIS models support 2003–2011. Final factors use all 2013–2025 training years; validation predictions use only the other 12 years.</p></section>
<section><h2>Whole-year validation</h2><p>All dates from the test year are excluded from fitting. Thirteen folds cover {v['sampleDays']:,} days. Zero-observation days remain in the test. MAE is mean absolute error in active cells per day. The baseline is the unadjusted input count.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Grid</th><th>Input</th><th>Baseline MAE</th><th>Adjusted MAE</th><th>Reduction</th><th>Years improved</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows}</tbody></table></div><p>At 4 km with Terra+Aqua input, monthly-total MAE falls from {v['monthlyTotals']['baseline']['mae']:.2f} to {v['monthlyTotals']['model']['mae']:.2f} active-cell days. The adjusted monthly bias is {v['monthlyTotals']['model']['bias']:.2f}, so the model still underestimates activity on average.</p><details><summary>Inspect every held-out year at 4 km</summary><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Baseline MAE</th><th>Adjusted MAE</th><th>Adjusted bias</th></tr></thead><tbody>{folds}</tbody></table></div></details></section>
<section><h2>Checks beyond the headline number</h2><p>We also tested a seasonal affine model and smaller case-study areas. Improvements were less consistent in those areas. The deployed adjustment is therefore restricted to the full study window. Candidate results are included in the repository rather than hidden.</p><p>A separate sensitivity test excludes all 211 UTC dates covered by listed NASA MODAPS Suomi NPP outage periods within the training span. At 4 km, MAE improvement remains {sens['maeImprovementPct']:.1f}% across {sens['sampleDays']:,} retained dates, with improvement in all 13 years. This exclusion is a coarse date mask, not a measurement of local cloud cover or observation availability.</p><p>A backward-transfer check fits 2018–2025 and tests 2013–2017. The 4 km Terra+Aqua adjustment reduces daily MAE by 44.2% in that earlier held-out period. This provides a limited test of transfer toward earlier years, although it cannot validate the pre-VIIRS period.</p></section>
<section><h2>What the error ranges mean</h2><p>Daily estimates use the 5th and 95th percentiles of held-out daily signed residuals. Monthly estimates use residuals of whole-month sums, so their ranges are not obtained by adding daily bounds. Bounds are clipped at zero and expanded to include the point estimate. They are descriptive error ranges, not guaranteed prediction intervals. Input counts outside the training range are marked in the downloadable calibration artifact.</p></section>
<section><h2>What remains unproven</h2><p>There is no VIIRS record before 2012 with which to validate these historical estimates. Applying the overlap-era relationship to 2001–2011 assumes it transfers across changes in fire behavior, viewing conditions and archive processing. The input and target also share MODIS observations. Error reduction does not demonstrate an equivalent reduction in missed real fires.</p><p>The model cannot reconstruct missing fire locations. No estimated cells are added to the map or raw-detection CSV. It does not estimate burned area, ignition counts or future fire risk. A multiplier gives a zero point estimate when its input is zero; this does not establish that fire was absent.</p></section>
<section><h2>Reproduce and inspect</h2><p>See <a href="https://github.com/Abhinav0905/ember-ledger">the source repository</a> for preparation, calibration, all candidate results and tests. The <a href="calibration.json.gz">complete calibration artifact (gzip JSON)</a> contains fitted factors, every yearly fold, historical daily and monthly estimates, and empirical ranges. The <a href="https://modaps.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/services/production/outages_suomi_npp.html">NASA outage table</a> supplies the sensitivity mask.</p></section><footer><a href="./">Explore the adjusted history ↗</a><a href="method.html">Read methods ↗</a></footer></main>'''
(OUT/'validation.html').write_text(page('Validation',validation))
slides='''<main class="presentation"><div class="slide-nav"><button id="previous-slide" class="secondary" type="button" aria-label="Previous slide">←</button><span id="slide-number" aria-live="polite"></span><button id="next-slide" class="secondary" type="button" aria-label="Next slide">→</button><button id="print-slides" class="secondary" type="button">Print slides</button></div>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">NASA SPACE APPS 2026</div><h1>Ember <em>Ledger</em></h1><p>Fire records change when satellites change. That makes it hard to tell whether an area had more burning activity or simply more detections.</p><p>A historical review tool for land managers: compare seasons, inspect sensor agreement and keep the evidence behind each comparison.</p><div class="slide-foot"><strong>Kumar Abhinav</strong><br>Harmonization of MODIS and VIIRS Hot Spots</div></section>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">THE PROBLEM, IN ONE DAY</div><h2>One day, two sensors</h2><div class="score"><div><strong>999</strong>MODIS detections</div><div><strong>3,755</strong>VIIRS detections</div></div><p>In the Dixie Fire study area on August 5, 2021, these observations occupy 98 shared-grid cells at 4 km. Both sensors saw 76 of them.</p><p>Each cell counts once per day. Sensor agreement stays visible, and the seasonal reference is 15 cells across 105 comparison days.</p></section>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">THE LONG RECORD</div><h2>Observed and estimated history</h2><p>Explore 1,086,752 retained NASA observations from 2001–2025 in and around Northern California.</p><p>The year-by-month calendar shows observed activity. A separate adjusted view estimates 2001–2011 totals for the full study region, with clear labels and empirical error ranges.</p><div class="slide-foot">Older estimates are modeled totals. Map cells and source downloads remain satellite observations.</div></section>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">TEST THE ADJUSTMENT</div><h2>Testing across 13 held-out years</h2><div class="score"><div><strong>44.8%</strong>lower daily count error</div><div><strong>13 / 13</strong>held-out years improved</div></div><p>At 4 km, a seasonal MODIS adjustment reduces mean absolute error against the combined record from 12.95 to 7.15 cells per day.</p><p>This tests agreement within the archive. Earlier-year transfer remains an assumption, and smaller-area models were less consistent.</p><div class="slide-foot">Full study window · 2013–2025 validation · Terra+Aqua input · <a href="validation.html">Inspect the tests ↗</a></div></section>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">A PROPOSED USE FOR LAND MANAGERS</div><h2>Evidence for a seasonal review</h2><p>A land manager can select periods for closer study without treating repeated satellite detections as separate fires.</p><ol><li>Find an unusual day in the calendar.</li><li>Check sensor agreement and whether its flag changes across 2, 4 and 8 km grids.</li><li>Download the review brief and source observations for follow-up.</li></ol><div class="slide-foot">Working prototype. No field deployment or measured operational benefit claimed. <a href="./">Try the atlas ↗</a></div></section>
<section class="slide"><div class="kicker">DATA, LIMITS &amp; NEXT STEPS</div><h2>Limits and next steps</h2><p>Clouds, overpass timing, outages and processing changes affect detections. Empty cells can contain fire. This is historical analysis, not a forecast or a burned-area estimate.</p><p>The record ends in 2025. Next steps are a current-year feed, coverage masks and independent tests before extending the adjustment to new regions.</p><div class="slide-foot">Data courtesy of NASA/LANCE FIRMS. Codex helped analyse code and build the interface.<br><a href="evidence.html">Evidence for the five criteria ↗</a> · <a href="method.html">Methods ↗</a></div></section><noscript>All six slides are displayed below the navigation controls.</noscript></main><script src="pitch.js"></script>'''
(OUT/'pitch.html').write_text(page('Six-slide project demo',slides,'<link rel="stylesheet" href="pitch.css">'))
print('Rendered methods, validation and six-slide pitch.')
